d to the non–muscle-invasive status described for high-grade disease in this data. [Data: Entities (5917)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is positioned as stages 2–4 and is linked to escalation to aggressive treatment\",\"explanation\":\"MUSCLE-INVASIVE BLADDER CANCER is defined as tumor invasion into the bladder’s muscle layer and is described as typically encompassing stages 2, 3, and 4. It is presented as having a more aggressive disease course than non–muscle-invasive bladder cancer and generally requiring more intensive treatment. [Data: Entities (5734, 2808)]\\n\\nA direct relationship connects MUSCLE-INVASIVE BLADDER CANCER to AGGRESSIVE (MORE INTENSE) TREATMENT, grounding the idea that muscle-layer invasion is a trigger for treatment escalation. In addition, the entity description states radical cystectomy is widely used and commonly described as the standard or most frequent surgical treatment option for this more advanced form. [Data: Entities (5734, 5865); Relationships (13326)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Stage 2 bladder cancer is explicitly classified as muscle-invasive but still confined to the bladder\",\"explanation\":\"STAGE 2 BLADDER CANCER is described as a muscle-invasive stage where the tumor has grown into the muscle layer of the bladder wall. It is also defined as confined to the bladder, without extension into the outer fatty tissue and without distant spread. [Data: Entities (5855)]\\n\\nThe relationship data explicitly links STAGE 2 BLADDER CANCER to MUSCLE-INVASIVE BLADDER CANCER, reinforcing that stage 2 belongs to the muscle-invasive category that drives more intensive management than superficial disease. [Data: Relationships (13343)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Stage 4 metastatic bladder cancer combines muscle invasion with systemic spread to distant sites\",\"explanation\":\"STAGE 4 METASTATIC BLADDER CANCER is defined as the metastatic stage where cancer has spread outside the bladder to distant sites in the body. The community links th